 \section{Conclusions}
 \label{sec:conclusion}
 
-We presented MgfrOFR, a symmetry-aware Mamba-based old film restoration framework that augments MambaOFR with explicit multi-granularity feature balancing. The Multi-Granularity Feature Extractor (MGFE) reorganizes propagated features into coarse-to-fine restoration-token branches and recalibrates the spatial stream through channel-wise feedback, while the Ranking-Aware Triplet Regularization (RATR) prevents these branches from collapsing into correlated representations during training.
+We presented MgfrOFR, a Mamba-based old film restoration framework that augments MambaOFR with explicit multi-granularity feature balancing. MGFE reorganizes propagated features into coarse-to-fine restoration-token branches and recalibrates the spatial stream through channel-wise feedback. RATR discourages branch collapse by penalizing correlated temporal-similarity rankings during training while preserving the baseline inference interface.
 
-Under the unified reproduction protocol on the SRWOV synthetic benchmark, MgfrOFR achieves the strongest full-reference scores among the tested baselines, improving PSNR by 1.14~dB over the strongest baseline MambaOFR (27.13~dB vs.\ 25.99~dB) with only 0.32\% additional parameters. On 216 real-world archival clips, MgfrOFR obtains the lowest NIQE and BRISQUE while remaining competitive on CLIP-IQA+. Because the real-world setting lacks ground truth, these no-reference gains should be interpreted as evidence of improved statistical naturalness rather than definitive proof of universal perceptual superiority. The ablation study confirms that MGFE and RATR are complementary: MGFE alone provides limited gains, whereas the full model improves fidelity and aggregate perceptual scores when branch diversity is explicitly enforced.
+The SRWOV results support the claim that coarse-to-fine branch balance improves old film restoration under heterogeneous degradation. Under the unified reproduction protocol on the SRWOV synthetic benchmark, MgfrOFR achieves the strongest full-reference scores among the tested baselines, improving PSNR by 1.14~dB over MambaOFR (27.13~dB vs.\ 25.99~dB) with only 0.32\% additional parameters. On 216 real-world archival clips, MgfrOFR obtains the lowest NIQE and BRISQUE while remaining competitive on CLIP-IQA+. Because the real-world setting lacks ground truth, these no-reference gains should be interpreted as evidence of improved statistical naturalness rather than definitive proof of perceptual superiority. The ablation study further shows that MGFE and RATR are complementary: MGFE alone provides limited gains, whereas the full model improves fidelity and aggregate perceptual scores when branch diversity is explicitly enforced.
 
-These results suggest that representation-level branch balance is a useful inductive bias for heterogeneous-degradation video restoration and practical archival digitization workflows. The main implication is that feature-space symmetry and temporally coordinated ranking behavior can improve restoration under degradation-induced asymmetry without changing the baseline inference interface. Several limitations remain: the method inherits the synthetic-to-real domain gap of REDS-based training; RATR adds a training-time cost that grows approximately quadratically with the batch-time descriptor set, increasing training time by approximately 7.6\% over MambaOFR under our setting; and evaluation is limited to the SRWOV benchmark and no-reference proxies on authentic footage. Future work will focus on adaptive branch activation conditioned on degradation severity, frequency-aware branch specialization that assigns structural recovery and texture restoration to dedicated branches, lighter ranking constructions such as selective anchor sampling, human-in-the-loop evaluation, and broader validation on additional archival benchmarks.
