 \subsubsection{When Does MgfrOFR Struggle?}
 \label{subsec:failure}
 
-While MgfrOFR achieves the strongest aggregate metrics, its advantage is not uniform across all degradation conditions. The ablation study (Table~\ref{tab:ablation}) reveals that MGFE alone degrades perceptual quality, and the visual ablations suggest that the full model is most useful when heterogeneous degradation is present, such as structural damage co-occurring with fine scratches in the same frame. On clips dominated by a single degradation type (e.g., uniform flicker without spatial damage), the multi-granularity decomposition can add representational overhead without proportional benefit, and the baseline or RATR-only variant may match the full model's fidelity.
