 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:introduction}
 
-Old film restoration is essential for preserving cinematic heritage, historical documentaries, and large archival collections whose original film stock continues to deteriorate over time. Digital restoration offers a practical route for revitalizing such material~\cite{iizuka2019deepremaster}, yet old footage is rarely degraded by a single defect type. Scratches, dust, blotches, flicker, blur, grain, and fading often co-occur within the same clip. These degradations span markedly different spatial scales, from large structural collapse to thin local artifacts, making old film restoration fundamentally different from standard video enhancement tasks. A central technical question is how to maintain feature-space symmetry across coarse structural recovery, regional texture repair, and fine artifact suppression when the observed degradation itself is asymmetric and heterogeneous.
+Old film restoration must recover visual content from footage affected by multiple interacting degradation types. Archival film stock often contains scratches, dust, blotches, flicker, blur, grain, and fading within the same clip, and these defects continue to accumulate as the original material deteriorates over time. Digital restoration therefore plays an important role in preserving cinematic heritage, historical documentaries, and large archival collections~\cite{iizuka2019deepremaster}. The technical difficulty is that these degradations occupy different spatial and temporal scales: large missing regions require global context, mid-scale stains require regional repair, and thin scratches or dust require local precision. In this paper, symmetry is used in a limited representation-level sense: the restoration model should keep coarse structural recovery, regional texture repair, and fine artifact suppression balanced even when the observed damage is heterogeneous and asymmetric.
 
-Learning-based old film restoration (OFR) has advanced rapidly through end-to-end video restoration frameworks. RTN introduced bidirectional recurrent propagation with learnable defect masking, but the restoration evidence still flows through one recurrent stream~\cite{wan2022rtn}. RRTN strengthened alignment with recursive transformer propagation, yet structural recovery and fine artifact suppression remain jointly encoded~\cite{lin2024rrtn}. MambaOFR brought state space models into OFR, improving efficiency and establishing the SRWOV benchmark, but it still treats different degradation scales through a single propagated feature pathway~\cite{mao2025mambaofr,gu2024mamba,guo2024mambair}. When heterogeneous signals compete in one latent space, coarse recovery may overwrite fine evidence, while local artifact suppression can distort global structure.
