-RATR prevents branch collapse by penalizing excessive ranking correlation between branches. A naive diversity loss could force descriptors to be orthogonal, but orthogonality does not ensure that branches organize restoration evidence differently across samples. The more relevant question is ranking behavior: when an anchor frame is compared with nearby positives and harder negatives, do different branches induce the same similarity order? If so, the branches remain redundant and the feature-space balance created by MGFE is lost. RATR formulates diversity as an anchor-centered ranking problem using Kendall's~\cite{kendall1938measure} rank correlation coefficient, made differentiable by DiffKendall~\cite{zheng2023diffkendall}. The workflow is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ratr_detail}.
